    "narrative": "Our lineup construction for today is anchored by **Corbin Carroll** (82.69) and **James Wood** (78.41), both of whom present elite upside against soft matchups. **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a favorable matchup against Painter, where his elite sprint speed overcomes a modest hurdle from a high-efficiency catcher to solidify his status as our primary stolen base threat. Meanwhile, **James Wood** is our strongest offensive play; batting leadoff against a vulnerable arm in Holmes, his elite 0.419 xwOBA drives a projection that is nearly 20 points above his base, making him the centerpiece of our stack. We are also leaning heavily into our platoon edges, specifically with **Vinnie Pasquantino** (65.33), who draws a significant +14% boost against the right-handed P\u00e9rez, and **Isaac Paredes** (62.40), who should exploit his platoon advantage against Martin to provide necessary power production.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Austin Riley** (50.22), who sat just outside our optimal configuration. While his base projection is solid, the combination of a lower-tier batting order position and a lack of stolen base utility kept him below our threshold for starting utility hitters like **Josh Naylor** (66.41), who commands a much more favorable environment against Sandoval. Conversely, we are keeping a close eye on **Seiya Suzuki** (47.89); his score is suppressed significantly by the elite stuff of Misiorowski, presenting a high-risk scenario where his output could deviate sharply from projections. While we have cleared our bench of non-contenders like **Travis Bazzana** and the injured **Tyler Soderstrom**, we must remain cautious of late-breaking weather or lineup changes, as any downgrade to the environments for **Brice Turang** or **Gabriel Moreno** could force a mid-day pivot to preserve our remaining game caps.",
